<Base title="About">
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    DS with X shows that you can do data science in whatever language makes sense to you, by building each
    technique from scratch in languages old and new.
  </p>
  <p>
    Libraries make data science easy to use and hard to understand. Writing k-means in COBOL or a regression
    in awk strips away the library and leaves only the idea, and seeing the same idea in many languages
    shows which parts are the math and which are just the language.
  </p>

  <h2>About the author</h2>
  <p>
    I started programming in the 7th grade, making shapes move across the screen from left to right. Through
    high school and college, I kept messing around with computers and programming languages. To me, each
    language is a different way to tell a computer what you want it to do.
  </p>
  <p>
    So it seemed strange, once I got into industry and grad school, to hear people say things like "you
    can't use that for data science" or "you must use this to be taken seriously."
  </p>
  <p>
    I'm building DS with X to show that yes, you can use this or that for data science. Whatever language
    you connect with, or that makes the most sense to you, is probably a language you can use for data
    science. That's why this site exists.
  </p>
</Base>
